          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "If a clinician writes diabetes in one place and cataract in another, does the \"with\" convention connect them?",
          "answer": "**Short answer: the retrieved sources don't say whether the \"with\" convention links diabetes and cataract, so I can't confirm it.**\n\nHere is what the sources do say:\n\n- The \"with\" convention is a general rule. Where the alphabetic index links two conditions with \"with\", the classification reads that as \"associated with\" or \"due to\" and presumes a relationship without the record stating it. The example given is type 2 diabetes with chronic kidney disease. (Source: *Coding type 2 diabetes: E11 and its complication subcategories*.)\n- The presumption holds only for pairings the index actually lists. It is overridden when the documentation says the two conditions are unrelated. (Source: *ICD-10-CM code structure and the conventions that govern selection*.)\n- Where documentation does not support more detail, the correct code is the unspecified one. Inferring a link the record does not contain is the error the convention is meant to prevent. (Same source.)\n\nWhat the sources do not say:\n\n- Whether the alphabetic index lists a \"with\" link between diabetes and cataract.\n- How coders handle the two conditions when they are written in different parts of the same record.\n\nThose answers would have to come from the current ICD-10-CM index and official coding guidelines, and a certified medical coder is the right person to apply them to a specific record.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
